<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Hyper-parameters used for training each model</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report the hyper-parameters used in Table <a href="#A5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ E.2 Training &amp; evaluation details ‣ Appendix E Implementation details ‣ Flow Matching for Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We use full 32 bit-precision for training CIFAR10 and ImageNet-32 and 16-bit mixed precision for training ImageNet-64/128/256. All models are trained using the Adam optimizer with the following parameters: <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay = 0.0, and <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1e{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1e{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>. All methods we trained (<span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, FM-OT, FM-Diffusion, SM-Diffusion) using identical architectures, with the same parameters for the the same number of Epochs (see Table <a href="#A5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ E.2 Training &amp; evaluation details ‣ Appendix E Implementation details ‣ Flow Matching for Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for details). We use either a constant learning rate schedule or a polynomial decay schedule (see Table <a href="#A5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ E.2 Training &amp; evaluation details ‣ Appendix E Implementation details ‣ Flow Matching for Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The polynomial decay learning rate schedule includes a warm-up phase for a specified number of training steps. In the warm-up phase, the learning rate is linearly increased from <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e{-8}</annotation></semantics></math> to the peak learning rate (specified in Table <a href="#A5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ E.2 Training &amp; evaluation details ‣ Appendix E Implementation details ‣ Flow Matching for Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Once the peak learning rate is achieved, it linearly decays the learning rate down to <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e{-8}</annotation></semantics></math> until the final training step.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When reporting negative log-likelihood, we dequantize using the standard uniform dequantization. We report an importance-weighted estimate using</p>
<table id="A5.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}p_{t}(x+u_{k}),\text{ where }u_{k}\sim{\mathcal{U}}(0,1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}p_{t}(x+u_{k}),\text{ where }u_{k}\sim{\mathcal{U}}(0,1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is in {0, …, 255} and solved at <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> with an adaptive step size solver <span id="A5.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dopri5</span> with <span id="A5.SS2.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">atol=rtol=1e-5</span> using the <span id="A5.SS2.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torchdiffeq</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen, 2018</a>)</cite> library. Estimated values for different values of <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> are in Table <a href="#A6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix F Additional tables and figures ‣ Flow Matching for Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When computing FID/Inception scores for CIFAR10, ImageNet-32/64 we use the TensorFlow <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_lstlisting">GAN</span> library <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/tensorflow/gan" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/tensorflow/gan</a></span></span></span>. To remain comparable to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal &amp; Nichol (2021)</a></cite> for ImageNet-128 we use the evaluation script they include in their publicly available code repository <span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://github.com/openai/guided-diffusion" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/openai/guided-diffusion</a></span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
